{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791032217,"nonce":"Us/PCI5YiOUI4cMOB1TleXt2w68=","title":"CI runners have fontconfig but no fonts, and usvg defaults to a font Linux lacks","message":"Two faults, found because a test asserting SVG `\u003ctext\u003e` draws turned\nthree CI jobs red at cf30312d.\n\n1. Every Linux job installs `libfontconfig1-dev` and no font package, so\n   `load_system_fonts()` finds nothing and `\u003ctext\u003e` still draws nothing\n   there. The test was right; the runner had no fonts.\n\n2. More importantly, the fix in cf30312d was incomplete for Linux\n   generally, not just CI. usvg's `Options::default()` sets\n   `font_family: \"Times New Roman\"`, which most Linux systems do not\n   have. An SVG with no `font-family` therefore resolved to no glyphs\n   even with a populated database.\n\nFixed by resolving the default family from what is actually installed,\npreferring the usual serif and sans faces and settling for any present\nface, and by setting fontdb's generic serif/sans families so a document\nasking for `sans-serif` resolves. `fonts-dejavu-core` added to the five\nLinux install blocks so the assertion is meaningful on CI.\n\nThe test also skips, loudly, when no fonts are installed: a bare\ncontainer cannot prove anything, and failing there says nothing about\nthe code. A second test covers the part CI can always verify — that the\ndatabase is attached for a document containing `\u003ctext\u003e` and withheld\nfrom a path-only one, so icons do not pay for a font scan.","files":null}]}